"""hidden_zones story pass (22 Nov 2026), to docs/quest_writing_standard.md. Where the giver's speech sat in the accept
line and the journal showed a summary or narration, the speech is now the description and the accept line a new short
beat; the rest were written fresh. Turn-ins untouched."""

STORY = {
    'hz_saltgrave_coins': (
        "My name's Maud, and I comb this sand for a living. The living's been thin since the barrows opened.\n\nThe dead out here were buried with their gold, and now they walk about with it in their pockets. They're old coins with a king on them that nobody remembers. I've got a buyer in the highlands who'll pay for every one, and I'm too old to argue with a wight about it.\n\nThey come up out of the west barrow and the east one. Bring me their coins and I'll make it worth your while. Mind your feet on the way over, because the shallows shift.",
        'Wipe the sand off before you hand them over. My buyer pays more for a face he can read.',
    ),
    'hz_saltgrave_lanterns': (
        "You've seen the lights out on the barrows at night. My mother called them corpse-lights. They aren't really lights at all. They're the dead who couldn't find their bodies, and they lead the living out into the water.\n\nI lost a boat to them last winter, and I'd like to stop losing things. Go out to the barrows, west and east, and put out the lanterns. The Brine-Wights keep them lit, so put them down as well.\n\nWhatever you do, don't follow a light anywhere it wants you to go.",
        'Go out at low tide. The bars are firmer, and the wights are slower out of the water.',
    ),
    'hz_saltgrave_king': (
        "There's a fourth barrow, out on its own to the north, and it belongs to the King. The others are his thanes, and they don't rest because he doesn't.\n\nHe walks the top of his barrow at low tide with his crown on, and he doesn't like visitors. I won't lie to you. I wouldn't go alone, and honestly I wouldn't go at all. But if anyone's going to put him back in the ground, it'll be somebody who brought friends.\n\nHis Drowned Barrow-Thanes stand round him. Go north past the middle bar, cut your way through them, and put the Brine King down for good.",
        "Watch the tide while you're out there. When it turns, the King's barrow becomes an island.",
    ),
    'hz_glass_shards': (
        "The basilisks here grow glass on their backs. They lie in the hot sand and it fuses to them a little more every year, until they're walking about wearing a fortune.\n\nI make blades from it. It takes the best edge you'll ever see, and it never rusts. But I've got one good leg left, and I'm keeping it.\n\nThe shard fields are west of the canyon mouth, where the sand shines. The basilisks will see you coming, so let them come to you. Bring me the shards off their backs.",
        'Pry them off with a blade, not your fingers. Fresh glass bites.',
    ),
    'hz_glass_kilns': (
        "Whatever burned this canyon never stopped. It's still up on the rise to the north, walking about on its own, and every time one of them comes down, the sand turns to glass under it and my camp gets smaller.\n\nI call them Kiln-Walkers. Water won't help you, but hitting them will. Go up the rise and break as many as you can, and I'll get a season's peace out of it.",
        "Don't stand on the glass they leave behind. It stays hot long after they've gone.",
    ),
    'hz_glass_blister': (
        "There's one basilisk up in the hollow at the north end that's more glass than beast. We call her Old Blister, and she's the reason I've only got one good leg.\n\nShe's been growing that hide since before I was born, and nothing I own will cut it. Don't go alone. Take people you trust, go up through the Glassback Basilisks round her, and when she opens her mouth, make sure you aren't standing in front of it.",
        'Wrap your hands before you go. Her glass cuts through gloves, and I learned that the hard way.',
    ),
    'hz_tangle_shallows': (
        "My name's Koji'ra. My grandmother told me about these shoals when I was small, and everybody laughed at her. I came anyway, and now I'm the one nobody would believe.\n\nThe snappers own the water and the stalkers own the trees, and between the two of them I can't get off this beach. The Mangrove Snappers are in the water round the landing, and the Shoal Stalkers hide in the tangles east and west of it.\n\nThin them out for me. Watch the branches over your head while you do it.",
        'Keep to the sand where you can. The roots in the water grab at boots.',
    ),
    'hz_tangle_sap': (
        "The trees out here bleed light. The guardians on the big island carry it inside them, and my grandmother said it came up from the roots of one tree that's older than the isles.\n\nI want to know whether she was right. Go out to the big island and bring me the sap from the guardians. They're slow, but they don't forget a face, so don't hang about.",
        'Bring it in a stoppered gourd. It glows through anything thinner, and things come to the light.',
    ),
    'hz_tangle_grandfather': (
        "There's one guardian on the big island that's bigger than all the rest. My grandmother called him Grandfather Mangrove, and she said he walks round the old tree the way a dog walks round a door it's guarding.\n\nI'd like to see that door. I'm not getting past him, and you won't either, not on your own. Bring friends, cut through the Rootbound Guardians, and put him down.",
        "Cut at his legs first. That's where his weight is, and he'll stumble.",
    ),
    'hz_idols_teeth': (
        "Fizzle Brassgut, treasure hunter, at your service! I've got a boat, a map and a lagoon full of drowned gods made of solid jade. What I can't do is get off this beach, because every single thing in the water wants to eat me.\n\nThe Lagoon Crocolisks and the Jade Serpents are all round the landing. You'll hear the crocs before you see them. Clear them out and I'll cut you in on the treasure. I mean it, partner.",
        "Wade, don't swim, if you can help it. Swimming's how you meet the serpents properly.",
    ),
    'hz_idols_jade': (
        "The trolls who drowned with the idols carry little bits of them. They chipped them off for luck, I reckon, and that didn't work out for them, did it?\n\nThe drowned ones on the western and eastern isles still have them. The hexers will try to put a curse on you, so don't let them get their hands up. Bring me enough shards and I can buy a bigger boat.",
        "Don't keep any shards for yourself, partner. You've seen how that worked out for them.",
    ),
    'hz_idols_zunjal': (
        "I've got bad news. The biggest idol, on the last isle up north, has a keeper. His name's Zun'jal. He drowned with the rest of them and he didn't let it slow him down one bit.\n\nHe's got his Drowned Hexers round him, and he isn't sharing. I'm not asking you to go alone. I'm asking you to bring every friend you've got, go north past the jade isles, and get that keeper out of the way.",
        "If he's wearing any jade, bring it. I'll split it with you fair and square.",
    ),
    'hz_caravan_dunes': (
        "Rizzo Dustwhistle's the name. My family's been looking for this caravan for four generations, and I'm the one who finally found it. Of course it was me.\n\nThe trouble is that it's full of scorpids, and of the people who were on it, who aren't really people any more. The Sandscour Scorpids are in the dunes by the landing, and the Caravan Ghouls are in the wagon graves to the west.\n\nClear me a way in. Watch the sand while you're at it, because it moves.",
        "If you find any of the old wagon wheels intact, mark where they are. I'd like to dig them out properly.",
    ),
    'hz_caravan_manifest': (
        "The caravan's ledger got torn up when they went under. Every guard on that caravan carried a piece of it, because the Master didn't trust anybody, not even his own family. The revenants in the drowned market, north of the dunes, still have the manifest pages, and they won't give them up without a fight. My grandfather spent his whole life guessing at what was on that list. Bring the pages back to me, and I'll finally know what's buried here, and where.",
        'Keep the pages flat and in order if you can. Half of reading a torn ledger is knowing which piece came next.',
    ),
    'hz_caravan_master': (
        "The ledger says the Master kept the good stuff in his own wagon at the very back. He's still in it, too. He still thinks he's delivering it.\n\nThe Sandstone Colossi at the buried gate keep people away from him, and he'll be harder than all of them put together. Bring a crew, go north through the gate to the back of the caravan, and put him to rest. Then I'll finally know what four generations of my family died looking for.",
        "Let him finish his delivery speech if he starts one. It's the least we can do.",
    ),
    'hz_kraal_hyenas': (
        "My name's Brannoc. The ground opened up under the Barrens and I came down on a rope to see why. The rope's gone now, and so is the way I came in.\n\nThe Pit Hyenas fell in the same way I did, and the Sinkhole Quillboar were already down here waiting. The hyenas roam the sinkhole floor, and the quillboar keep to the thornwalls west of it. Clear them out for me so I can think.",
        "Watch the hyenas' ears. They twitch just before the pack moves.",
    ),
    'hz_kraal_bones': (
        "The thornspeakers down here carry bones carved with prayers. I want to know who they're praying to in a hole like this, because something's answering them.\n\nThey keep to the thornwalls in the west and the shrine-pool to the north. They'll pray at you when you get close, and it's best to ignore it. Bring me the bones and I'll read them.",
        "Don't answer if they pray at you. I'm not sure what happens when you do.",
    ),
    'hz_kraal_mother': (
        "Her seat is at the north end, above everything else down here. Her dead walk round it, and she walks among them, or what's left of her does.\n\nShe's the reason the ground opened. I'm sure of it now. Bring friends, go north to her seat, and get through the Kraal Bonewalkers that guard it. She'll sing, and the thorns will listen to her. If you put her down, maybe the ground closes again.",
        "Cover your ears when she sings. The thorns aren't the only things that listen.",
    ),
    'hz_glade_wisps': (
        "My name's Ithrael. My grandmother put the seal on this glade and she never spoke about it. I've come to read what she wouldn't say.\n\nThe Gloomwisps lead people off the path and leave them there to wander. They keep to the shadowed path near where you came in. Put them out before they do the same to me.",
        'Stay on the path even when it looks wrong. The wrong-looking way is usually the right one here.',
    ),
    'hz_glade_sentinels': (
        "The Sentinels my grandmother left here are still on watch. They died on watch, and they don't know they're allowed to stop.\n\nThe satyrs came because the Sentinels can't see them any more. The Shades of the Sentinel stand in the silent grove to the west, and the Moonless Satyrs have taken the twisted hollow to the east. Free the Sentinels, and put an end to the satyrs.",
        'Speak to them before you strike, if you can. Tell them their watch is over.',
    ),
    'hz_glade_priestess': (
        "I've read it all now. A priestess here prayed to Elune for something she shouldn't have, Elune turned away, and the moon hasn't shone on this glade since.\n\nShe's still in the sealed shrine at the north end, still praying, and the Shades of the Sentinel still guard her door. She won't stop praying long enough to talk. Go with friends, and give her an answer.",
        "If she asks you what Elune said, tell her the truth. She's waited long enough for it.",
    ),
    'hz_storm_rams': (
        "Halvard Grey, and I chase storms. This one never moves, which makes it either the easiest storm in the world to study or the most dangerous. The Thunderhide Rams charge anything that moves on the crag path, and the Stormcrag Harpies nest in the east ring. I need both of them out of the way so I can set up my rods. I've lost two rods to the rams already, and one to a harpy who seemed to think it was a perch.",
        "If a ram paws the ground, step aside rather than back. They can't turn once they've started.",
    ),
    'hz_storm_shards': (
        "The stormlings up here carry shards with lightning still inside them. If I can read enough of them, I can tell you what's calling the storm.\n\nThey're thickest on the thunder table to the north. They'll shock you, and that's how you'll know it's working. Bring me their shards.",
        "Hold them by the wrappings, not the stone. You'll feel why.",
    ),
    'hz_storm_stone': (
        "The stone at the top calls the storm, and the wardens in the rings keep anyone from getting near it. Somebody made them for that. Somebody wanted this storm here.\n\nGo north to the very top of the crag, get through the Stone-Circle Wardens and break the stone. Bring friends who don't mind being struck by lightning. Don't wear anything metal. I'm joking, mostly.",
        'If you hear humming in the rings, keep going. It means the wardens have noticed you.',
    ),
    'hz_orchard_rows': (
        "My name's Marta. My family grew apples here before the plague. I died in that house over there, and when I woke up I walked straight back to it. It's funny how that works.\n\nThe Orchard Scarecrows don't know me any more, and the Orchard Ghouls never did. They're in the grey rows west of the gate. The scarecrows only look like they're standing still, so be careful.",
        "If one of the scarecrows turns its head, it's already decided. Don't wait for it to move.",
    ),
    'hz_orchard_cider': (
        "The pickers in the cider house are still pressing apples, and the apples are plagued. Somebody's going to drink that one day, and I'd rather it never reached a cup. Go east to the cider house and stop the Plague Pickers. The Rotfruit Bats eat the fallen fruit and carry it off, so they need stopping too. My mother pressed the first cider in that house, and I'd like it to stop making poison in her name.",
        'The bats are everywhere, so look up as often as you look ahead.',
    ),
    'hz_orchard_bramley': (
        "My father made the first scarecrow in this orchard and named it Bramley after the apple. It stands under the old tree to the north now with a scythe, and the plague's in it, and it won't stop.\n\nPlease put it down. Take friends, because it was built to keep things away, and it's good at it. The Orchard Scarecrows round it will come at you first.",
        "If the old tree's still standing when you're done, leave it. That tree's the only thing here still alive.",
    ),
    'hz_root_listen': (
        "My name's Ash'ka, and I'm Koji'ra's grandmother. I told her about this tree when she was small, and then I came to find it myself, and it kept me. I don't mind. It's beautiful down here.\n\nThe Darkspear who followed me down stopped listening to the roots and started speaking for them, and they're wrong about what the tree wants. Go through the amber hollows and out along the roots, quiet the Lost Rootspeakers, and clear the Heartwood Spiders out of the dark galleries between.",
        "Go quietly down there. The roots listen, and they don't like to be shouted over.",
    ),
    'hz_root_amber': (
        "In the heartwood, there's a pool of sap that woke up. We call her Amberheart. She was meant to feed the tree, and she's started keeping it all for herself, and the tree's getting hungry.\n\nYou'll find the heartwood in the middle of everything, and you'll know it by the light. She'll seal you in amber if she can, so bring friends who can cut you out.",
        'Keep moving through the heartwood. Anything standing still there turns gold.',
    ),
    'hz_root_father': (
        "At the very bottom of the tree, which is the top of everything down here, the oldest root of all has woken up. We call him the Root-Father. He was here before the isles were, and he's angry that anyone found him.\n\nOld Gnarl the Unburied, out in the knotted dark to the west, has been feeding him. Go up through the knotted deep to the Root-Father's bed and put them both back to sleep. Don't go alone. Nobody should go anywhere alone down here.",
        "When they're both asleep, come back up slowly. The tree will be listening to see if you're gentle.",
    ),
    'hz_vault_quiet': (
        "I was the librarian here, before the Bloom and before the Scourge, and before most things you'd know about. I stayed because somebody has to know where everything is.\n\nThe Palimpsest Wraiths are what's left of the books the Bloom wrote over, and the Erasures are what's left of the people who read them. They're in the outer stacks. Go round, because everything down here goes round, and give them rest.",
        'Walk softly between the shelves. The wraiths drift toward footsteps.',
    ),
    'hz_vault_pages': (
        "The scholars who drowned in the ink carry pages that have been scraped clean. But a scraped page remembers, if you know how to read it.\n\nThey walk the inner shelves. You'll have to find the way in, because the gate into each ring is on a different side. Bring me their pages and I'll read what was written on them first.",
        "Bring the pages blank side up. I'd rather you didn't look at what's coming through.",
    ),
    'hz_vault_keeper': (
        "The Keeper has been trying to finish the first page for longer than there have been people to read it. The Last Editor, at the heart of the vault, strikes out every word he writes. They'll never finish, and while they try, nothing down here can rest. The Editor sits at the heart of the rings, and the Keeper of the First Page reads alone beyond the top of the vault. I've shelved every book in this place, and I'd like to see that last one closed, so end it for both of them.",
        'Take everyone you trust. Neither of them will thank you, but both of them will stop.',
    ),
    'hz_pines_webs': (
        "My name's Ilsabet. I came here to study the mere, and I've stayed because I can't bear to leave it unfinished.\n\nThe Gloom Spiders in the west wood hang their kills in the pines, and then the kills come down again on their own. I'd like fewer of both. They're west of the track where the pines are thickest, so look up before you look down.",
        "Cut down anything hanging in the pines as you go. I'd rather it stayed down.",
    ),
    'hz_pines_water': (
        "The wraiths at the mere are made of the water itself. If you break one apart, there's a little left that won't drain away, and that's what I need. It remembers things, and I want to know what.\n\nThey keep to the mere and the shrine north of it. Bring me the water, and don't drink any of it. I know you weren't going to.",
        'Seal each vial the moment you fill it, because mere-water climbs.',
    ),
    'hz_pines_weeping': (
        "The worgen here don't hunt. They walk the track and weep, and if you get between one of them and the water it'll tear you apart to get past. The Rotbark Treants in the drowned copse east of the mere have started doing the same.\n\nI've read enough to know they aren't the cause, but they're in the way of it. Clear the Weeping Worgen off the track and the treants out of the copse. They won't stop crying, so try not to let it get to you.",
        'Stop your ears with moss if the crying gets too much. It helps a little.',
    ),
    'hz_pines_widow': (
        "She drowned herself in the mere after the pines died, and she took the wood with her. That's what the vials showed me. Everything here weeps because she does.\n\nShe rises out of the water when the fog's thickest, with her Mere Wraiths round her. I can't fight her, and you can't either, not alone. Bring people who aren't afraid of crying things, and don't listen when she says your name.",
        "If she says your name, keep walking toward her. That's the last thing she'll expect.",
    ),
    'hz_springs_beads': (
        "The pools are wrong. They were always hot, but since the Integration they're hotter than any spring I ever logged, and the colours have started moving outward a little every week. The steam things and the mud things carry beads of sinter in them, set from the same water. The scaldlings are round the painted pools and the steaming mouth, and the mud shamblers keep to the mud pots in the north-west. Bring me a handful of the beads, and I'll work out how hot the water runs below and how fast it's rising.",
        'Let the beads cool before you pocket them. Fresh sinter burns right through cloth.',
    ),
    'hz_lanternmere_jars': (
        "When a wisp goes out, it leaves a little cold light behind, and the light keeps trying to go somewhere. Every one of them tries to go the same way, north up the causeway. I've watched them from the shore for weeks, and not one has gone any other way. Kill the wisps over the shallows and scoop up what's left of their light. Put it in jars and bring it here, and we'll see where it wants to go.",
        "Put the lids on fast. They're quick, and they don't like being kept.",
    ),
    'hz_lanternmere_lamplighter': (
        "There used to be a man who kept the lanterns on the causeway, so travellers wouldn't wander into the water. When he died, he kept doing it, except now he lights them to lead people in, and every wisp on the mere is one of his lamps. He keeps the lanterns at the top of the causeway, north of the meres, and you'll see his light first, because I've watched it from my camp every night. The Amani Barrow-Shades guard the top of the causeway for him. Take them down, and then The Lamplighter, and take friends, because he's had a long time to get good at it.",
        "Don't look straight into his lantern. It's how he gets people walking toward the water.",
    ),
    'hz_winter_ice': (
        "The glacier is moving. Glaciers do, but this one moves like it's deciding to, and it's coming south a few yards every night. I've measured it every night since I got here, and it's already buried half the camp. If it reaches the Lanternmere, it'll push through into the Plaguelands, and an ice age will start in Lordaeron. The ice front is in the north-east, past the frozen lake, and the Woolly Rhinoceros herd grazes right up against it, so get past them and break The Advancing Ice at the heart of it, and bring every friend you have.",
        'Keep moving out on the ice. If you stand still too long, it starts to take you.',
    ),
    'hz_sunward_road': (
        "Lirael Brightwater, ranger of the waystation, and the last one still breathing. When the Scourge came down the road, the Magister sealed us all inside the wardstones, and the afternoon's never ended since. The Sunward Lynxes and the Sunward Mana Wyrms have gone strange in the endless light, and they hunt anything that walks the road. The lynxes keep to the long grass beside the road, and the wyrms drift where the light's brightest. Thin them out, so the road's safe to walk again.",
        "Don't wait for dusk. It isn't coming.",
    ),
    'hz_sunward_shards': (
        "The ward's failing at the north end, and I don't know how to mend it. The Arcane Remnants carry shards of the wardstones in them, bits of the spell that broke loose. They drift through the waystation and the glimmering wood, and they'll throw the spell at you. Bring me some of the shards. If I can read what's left in them, I can tell how long the ward's got.",
        'Hold the shards apart from each other. When they touch, they try to remember being whole.',
    ),
    'hz_raincatch_snappers': (
        "I'm Old Ma'luu, and I climbed up here to pray for rain when I was a girl, at the shrine the first climbers built. The rain came, and it never stopped, and the pools filled every step of the old terraces.\n\nI've lived on the lips between them ever since. The Mossback Snappers lie in the moss at the water's edge, and the Stilt-Leg Herons spear anything that sits still too long. Thin them out, so an old woman can walk her own terraces.",
        "Watch the moss at the edge. It looks like ground, and it isn't.",
    ),
    'hz_raincatch_sparks': (
        "The Rain Darters over the drowned shrine are made of the rain, I'm sure of it. They flit over it on the middle terrace, halfway up the pools. When one goes out, it leaves a drop behind that won't stop falling. Bring me some of those rain sparks. If I can hold the rain in my hands, maybe I can finally ask it to stop.",
        'Cup them in both hands. They slip through fingers faster than water does.',
    ),
    'hz_raincatch_priest': (
        "The priest who taught me prayed for rain at that shrine for forty years. When the rain came, he stayed to give thanks, and the water rose over him, and he went on praying. He's still down there, all moss and water, and he still thinks we want it to rain.\n\nThe Rain Darters circle him like moths. Go out to the shrine and let him stop. Take friends, because he won't want to.",
        "Tell him the rain's been enough. He might listen to someone new.",
    ),
    'hz_raincatch_croak': (
        "There's a toad in the lily shallows of the top pool as big as my lean-to. I call her Grandmother Croak, and for years I didn't mind her, because she ate the eels.\n\nNow she's eating the herons, and the snappers, and last week she had a go at me. I'm too old to be eaten by a toad. Please see to it.",
        "She lunges from the lilies. Keep out of reach of her tongue and you'll be fine.",
    ),
    'hz_kapok_howlers': (
        "Dr. Luana Ferreira. I'm a canopy ecologist from Manaus, back home, and this is my canopy, the crowns of the kapok trees over the flooded forest. I climbed a tower one morning to count birds, and when I came down the tower was gone and the forest was here, in this tree, on this world.\n\nIt's a small piece of forest and it's getting crowded. The Goliath Birdeaters have run out of birds and started on everything else, and the Howler Monkeys have gone mad with it. Thin both out, please, gently if you can.",
        'Leave any nests with young in them. The crown needs the next lot as much as it needs fewer of these.',
    ),
    'hz_kapok_pods': (
        "The kapoks are seeding, and the seeds are the only proof I've got that this forest came from Earth. The Howler Monkeys steal the pods to line their beds, round the howler crown and the first crown. Look for the white fluff, because that's how you'll know which ones have them. Bring me some of the kapok pods, and I'll plant them on the terraces below. If a kapok grows in this world's soil, then some part of home can live here too.",
        "Don't let the fluff blow away, because every seed I lose is a tree that never grows.",
    ),
    'hz_kapok_boiuna': (
        "The west fork came through drowned in the flood, and something came with it. The people on the river back home tell stories about the Boiuna, a snake so big it's mistaken for an island, the mother of the river. I always thought it was a story.\n\nShe's not a story, and she's eating the crown. The Green Anacondas round the flood are her children. Please, go carefully, and take every friend you have.",
        "Watch the water for an island that moves. That's her, and she's bigger than she looks.",
    ),
    'hz_kapok_jaguar': (
        "There's a jaguar on the emergent crown, the tallest one, who's older than any jaguar I've ever seen. Grey round the muzzle, scarred all over, and clever. I've been watching her for a year.\n\nShe's started hunting the camp. I'd give anything not to ask you this, but I'd like to go on living. Please make it quick.",
        "She'll come from above, out of the crown. Look up before you step onto any branch.",
    ),
    'hz_stormroost_nests': (
        "I'm Kelsey Draper. I chased tornadoes across Oklahoma for nine years, out of Norman, and I woke up here three years ago under the only storm I've ever seen that doesn't move.\n\nWhoever built these nests left a long time ago, and the birds moved in. The Roost Thunderbirds throw lightning off their wings, and the Roost Raptors dive on anything the lightning knocks down. Thin them out so I can get to my instruments without being struck twice a day.",
        'Keep your weapon low when the thunderbirds pass over. They aim for anything raised.',
    ),
    'hz_stormroost_feathers': (
        'There are things drifting round the old nests that look like people made of wind. When one comes apart, it leaves a feather behind, and the feather hums.\n\nI recorded one on my phone before the battery died, and the hum matched the thunder to the second. Bring me six of them. If the feathers and the storm are singing the same note, I want to know who taught them.',
        'Keep them apart in your pack. Two humming feathers together give me a headache.',
    ),
    'hz_stormroost_stormcaller': (
        "I went up to the tip last night with my binoculars. There's someone standing at the altar under the thunderhead, feathered like the old carvings and grey as the cloud, and they're singing. They've been singing since long before I got here.\n\nThey're holding the storm in place, and the storm's tearing the island apart a little more every week. Go up and let them finish the song. Take friends, because the Thunderhead Squalls won't let you near quietly.",
        "Let them finish the verse they're on if you can. It feels wrong to cut a song off mid-line.",
    ),
    'hz_stormroost_mother': (
        "There's one thunderbird in the old nests at the top of the west vane that's bigger than the rest put together. I call her the Thunder-Mother, and every bird on this island came out of her clutch.\n\nShe's taken to diving on my camp. I've been in a lot of storms and I've never been scared like that. Please go up and deal with her.",
        "Take shelter between her dives. She has to climb back up each time, and that's your moment.",
    ),
    'hz_orrery_machinery': (
        "I'm Tessaly Ombrine, of the House of Measures. My house has spent four hundred years measuring the sky, and nobody in it has ever written down that this place exists. I found it chasing a number that wouldn't add up.\n\nIt's a model of the heavens you can walk on, and it's still running. The Orbit Wardens keep the rings turning, and they don't like being measured. The Star Moths are drawn to the light. Clear some of both so I can work.",
        "Step over the rails, never on them. The rings don't stop for anyone.",
    ),
    'hz_orrery_stars': (
        "Every so often a star falls into the rings and keeps on burning. They're not stars, of course, but they're made of the same crystal as the moons, and they burn exactly as long as the model says a real one would. They come down on the moons, and the red moon on the outer orbit catches the most. Bring me chips from six of them. If I can date the crystal, I can work out when the orrery was set running.",
        "Chip from the edge, not the middle. The middle's still burning.",
    ),
    'hz_orrery_keeper': (
        "There's a construct on the sun dais that winds the whole orrery, and it's started winding it faster. The rings are a quarter turn ahead of where they were when I arrived, and the stars are falling twice as often.\n\nI don't know what happens when it reaches the end of the count, and I'd rather not find out by standing here. Go and stop it for me. The Measuring Drones work for it, so expect company, and take friends.",
        "Note the position of the rings when it stops. I'll want to know where the count ended.",
    ),
    'hz_orrery_moon': (
        "One of the moons on the outer orbit has woken up. It's the pale one at the north end, and it isn't staying on its moon any more. It drifts round the rings at night and anything it shines on goes cold.\n\nI'd like to measure it, honestly, but I'd like to keep my fingers more. Please put it out.",
        'Keep a fire lit near you while you fight it. The cold it gives off goes straight to the bone.',
    ),
    'hz_cloudwreck_salvage': (
        "I'm Ressa Vane. I was a quartermaster in the Bastions until I worked out where the ships went when they didn't come back, and then I stopped being one. Nobody comes looking for a deserter down here.\n\nI live off what I pull out of the wrecks, and so does everything else. The Hull Crabs strip the brass and the Rigging Stalkers hunt in the ropes. Clear enough of both that I can work the near wrecks again.",
        "Leave the brass the crabs have stacked. They'll guard a pile harder than they'll guard themselves.",
    ),
    'hz_cloudwreck_tags': (
        "The crews never left their ships. The Lost Sky-Sailors still walk the decks as if the ships were flying, and every one of them still wears the tag the Ascendancy stamps for its sailors.\n\nThe Bastions write lost crews off as deserters, so their families don't get paid. Bring me their tags. I kept the ledgers once, and I know how to make the right people read them.",
        'Read the names out when you take them. Somebody aboard should hear them said.',
    ),
    'hz_cloudwreck_admiral': (
        "The old flagship at the far north end of the shelf is older than any ledger in the Bastions. Its admiral never gave the order to abandon ship, and so nobody aboard ever did. He's still on the quarterdeck, past the long shelf, and every crew that's fallen since answers to him. If The Unsunk Admiral is gone, they can stop, and I'd like them to stop. The Fallen Gunners still man his cannon, so go carefully, and take friends.",
        "If his log's on the quarterdeck, bring it. I'd like to know the last order he gave.",
    ),
    'hz_cloudwreck_eater': (
        "Something lives under the long shelf that eats wrecks. I've watched it come up through the cloud and take half a hull in one bite, crew and all. It ate my winch last week.\n\nI'd very much like my winch back, but I'll settle for it not eating anything else. Please go and see to it.",
        "Stay off the edge of the shelf when it comes up. It takes whatever's standing closest.",
    ),
    'hz_tepui_summit': (
        "I'm Mireya Lanz, a mountain guide from Santa Elena de Uairen, in Venezuela, and this is Roraima, or the top of it. I've led more than three hundred groups up the ramp. I was up here with eleven trekkers the night it happened, and in the morning the clouds were below us and the mountain wasn't under us any more.\n\nThe trekkers have all found their way to somewhere better, thank God, and I decided to stay. The Summit Tarantulas and the Labyrinth Scorpions have got far too big up here. Thin them out so I can walk my mountain again.",
        'Mind the cracks in the rock. Some of them go down further than the mountain used to.',
    ),
    'hz_tepui_nectar': (
        "The pitcher plants on the summit are called Heliamphora, sun pitchers, and they only grow on tepuis. Back home they were the size of a coffee cup. Up here, since the change, the Sun-Pitchers walk.\n\nBring me nectar from six of them. If I know what's in it, I'll know what's making them grow, and whether it's going to stop.",
        "Don't drink any of it, however good it smells. The plants know what they're doing with it.",
    ),
    'hz_tepui_mother': (
        "The big one in the jacuzzis is taller than a house. She fills with rain every afternoon and pours it out over the rock, and wherever it runs another Sun-Pitcher comes up. In a year there won't be a bare stone on the summit.\n\nI love these plants. I've shown them to three hundred groups. But this isn't how they grow, and she has to go. Take friends, because she knows you're coming.",
        "Burn the rain channels she uses once she's down. Otherwise her seedlings will keep coming up.",
    ),
    'hz_tepui_harpia': (
        "A harpy eagle came up with the mountain. They live in the forest round the foot of it back home, and she must have been hunting near the top when it happened. She's grown since, like everything has.\n\nShe sits on the prow at the north end and takes whatever walks out onto the rock. Last week she took a cloud wolf as big as a pony. I'd rather it wasn't me next. Please make it quick.",
        "She'll stoop from the sun. Keep it at your back if you can.",
    ),
}
